g the underlying trauma is not  only clinically unhelpful but it  actively can do more harm and this is  why the obesity guidelines now suggest  that it's more important to focus on the  root cause of weight gain and  sustainable behaviors rather than  suggesting that individuals just eat  less to weigh less it doesn't really  work like that now in addition to mental  and emotional challenges to weight loss  we also need to consider the systemic  barriers at play which brings me to my  next point shows like this dismiss the  societal factors that contribute to  obesity the common narrative around  obesity is that it's a consequence of  someone's choices and is therefore an  individual's responsibility to fix by  making better choices however this  assumption about the cause of obesity  dismisses all of the systemic factors  that are much stronger predictors of  obesity than any single meal in other  words someone's external environment and  socioeconomic status can play a far more  significant role in their health  outcomes than simply their lifestyle we  grew up very poor we grew up with fast  food mostly but there was five kids so  our mom worked three jobs i got to be  honest i never bought really healthy  food in the past but this time i'm gonna  buy some tomato onion avocados  cucumbers lettuces this experience is  not uncommon among folks from low-income  households who have limited access to  nutrient-dense foods in fact research  has shown that fast food outlets are  more accessible in low-income  communities whereas access to  supermarkets and grocery stores with a  variety of fresh foods are more limited  it's therefore no surprise that living  in a low-income community with greater  access to convenience foods is a strong  predictor of obesity amy herself has  even acknowledged that one of the  challenges she's experienced in trying  to eat healthier comes down to  differences in affordability and  accessibility where purchasing a burger  for a buck is much easier than buying a  salad for five dollars now many have  been quick to criticize her remark as an  excuse to continue eating calorie dense  foods but given the statistics and her  family's economic history i think it's a  very real valid barrier to behavioral  change moreover we also know that  poverty induces scarcity mentality  especially when the experience of  poverty is deep seeded from childhood  which can then perpetuate binge eating  behaviors in other words coming from a  place of caloric or food insecurity  causes you to overcompensate later on in  life i mean a lot of us who had  grandparents in the war can attest to  their butter hoarding tendencies it's a  similar phenomenon memories of  insecurity imprint very very hard so now  we've got childhood weight stigma  childhood trauma poverty induced  scarcity mentality and mobility  challenges all making it very difficult  to break the cycle and this speaks to  just how complex obesity is and why  punitive advice like this if you care  about something bad enough it'll be a  priority and if you don't it won't be a  priority it's really that simple it's  actually not that simple when you  consider the bigger picture and it also  completely ignores the very real and  valid reasons why following any type of  diet protocol can be extremely  challenging which brings me to my next  point restrictive diets like those shown  on the show come with significant risk  now in order for amy and tammy to  qualify for bariatric surgery they have  to get their weight down to  550 pounds now this is a common  recommendation that's necessary to  qualify for surgery as excess fat around  the abdomen and liver can increase the  risk of complications so to help  facilitate their preliminary weight loss  they enlist the support of a dietitian  the dietitian wants us to throw out all  the junk food and all the stuff we can  eat  but drinking saudis eating cookies on  this diet you're supposed to drink  protein shakes twice a 